# The built bundle is committed, because installing this plugin straight from GitHub needs it —
# and that makes every pair of branches that touches the code conflict on a minified file. Git
# would try to interleave two builds line by line, which can only produce a bundle matching no
# source at all.
#
# `merge=ours` keeps the current branch's bundle instead; `npm run build` then regenerates it from
# the merged sources, and CI fails the branch if that was forgotten (the "Bundle matches the
# sources" step in .github/workflows/ci.yml). The check is what makes this safe rather than quiet.
#
# **`ours` is not a built-in driver** — verified the hard way, the conflict came back regardless
# until it was declared. Git looks it up in the clone's own config, which no repository file can
# set (a repo that could define merge commands would be a repo that runs code on clone). Hence the
# `prepare` script in package.json, which registers it on `npm install`; by hand it is:
#
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
main.js merge=ours

# Same reasoning in the demo fixtures: a `.canvas` is JSON that Obsidian writes, and a three-way
# merge of it yields a file the app declines to open.
*.canvas merge=ours
